      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Indigenous travelers crossed the Allegheny near East Hickory each spring to Oil Creek, where they gathered natural oil, boiled maple sugar, and built bark canoes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/east_hickory_pennsylvania",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Hickory Valley Railroad ran from East Hickory in the late 1800s, linking local logging camps to wider markets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/east_hickory_pennsylvania",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Forest County, which contains East Hickory, was formed on April 11 1848 from Jefferson County and expanded in 1866 to include parts of Venango County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
